    // index
    "ix.0": "High School · US History Since 1877 · §113.41 · Problem-Based Learning",
    "ix.1": "The Progressive Era — Should We Regulate Big Business?",
    "ix.2": "A Problem-Based Learning unit. Students step into the industrial America of 1906, take on a stakeholder's point of view, and work a real, ill-structured question about business, government, workers, and reform — building from surface to deep to transfer learning. The teacher is a guide, not the answer key.",
    "ix.3": "<b>Driving question:</b> It is 1906. Industrial America is booming — and breaking. Should government step in to regulate big business and protect workers and consumers, and if so, <i>how far</i>?",
    "ix.4": "The three-phase path (do them in order)",
    "ix.5": "Problem-solving is a <b>transfer</b> move — it only works once students have knowledge to reason with. So the problem in Phase 3 is deliberately <b>gated</b> behind Phases 1 and 2.",
    "ix.6": "Build the knowledge",
    "ix.7": "Vocabulary, key facts about industrialization's problems and the muckrakers, a jigsaw read, and data/map work on reform. ~1–2 periods.",
    "ix.8": "Connect &amp; organize",
    "ix.9": "Problems–reforms concept map, primary sources, competing points of view, and a structured argument. ~1–2 periods.",
    "ix.10": "Solve the problem",
    "ix.11": "Meet the problem, take a stakeholder role, investigate, propose &amp; defend a reform stance, debrief. ~2–4 periods.",
    "ix.12": "⛔ <b>Gate:</b> don't open the Phase 3 problem until students have finished the surface and deep activities. Meeting the problem too early turns inquiry into guessing.",
    "ix.13": "Big idea &amp; objectives",
    "ix.14": "<b>Big idea:</b> Societies weigh <b>economic freedom, public welfare, and the reach of government</b> when they decide whether and how to regulate powerful private interests — and the choices Americans made in the Progressive Era reshaped business, democracy, and public health while leaving out or harming some of the very people reform claimed to serve.",
    "ix.15": "<b>Know:</b> the problems industrialization created — monopolies, unsafe factories and food, child labor, urban poverty, political machines — the muckrakers who exposed them, and the reforms that followed (the Pure Food and Drug and Meat Inspection Acts of 1906; the 16th–19th Amendments).",
    "ix.16": "<b>Understand:</b> the same reform question looks profoundly different from each stakeholder's point of view — journalist, business owner, labor organizer, politician, immigrant worker, and consumer alike.",
    "ix.17": "<b>Do:</b> run the problem-solving and decision-making process end to end — identify the problem, gather information, list and weigh options, choose and defend a solution, and evaluate it (§113.41(c)(31)(B)).",
    "ix.18": "Standards this unit is aligned to (§113.41)",
    "ix.19": "TEKS SE",
    "ix.20": "Where it lives in the unit",
    "ix.21": "Effects of reform and third-party movements; impact of Progressive Era reforms — Surface facts, Deep concept map",
    "ix.22": "Analyze the impact of Progressive Era reforms (initiative, referendum, recall, direct election of senators, suffrage) — Surface, Deep, Transfer",
    "ix.23": "Source analysis, points of view, claim + evidence — Deep &amp; Transfer",
    "ix.24": "Communicate findings in written and oral form — Deep argument, Transfer brief",
    "ix.25": "<b>The problem-solving &amp; decision-making process — the entire Transfer phase</b>",
    "ix.26": "Teacher prep &amp; materials",
    "ix.27": "Print the <a href=\"facilitator-guide.html\">facilitator guide</a> (teacher moves, map of possibilities, role cards, debrief prompts) and the <a href=\"assessment.html\">assessment pack</a> (rubric + individual-in-group).",
    "ix.28": "Chart paper or a projected space for three shared columns: <b>Hunches · Know · Need-to-Know</b> (the KWHL chart).",
    "ix.29": "Vetted sources for investigation (district-approved). Suggested public-access sources are listed in the facilitator guide.",
    "ix.30": "No logins, no accounts — every page runs in the browser. Student writing and products live on paper or in tools your district already vets.",
    "ix.31": "⚠️ <b>Honest history:</b> this unit teaches the Progressive movement's real gains — food and drug safety, direct democracy, woman suffrage — alongside its blind spots, directly, factually, and respectfully (TEC §28.0022). Many reforms excluded or actively harmed Black Americans, and segregation deepened in this same era; some Progressives embraced eugenics and immigration restriction. Progress and injustice ran side by side.",
    "ix.32": "▶ Start Phase 1 — Surface",
    "ix.33": "🧑‍🏫 Facilitator guide",
